course: dictionary
lesson: 13303
title: Gravitation: the pull that holds us down
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: Gravitation is a pull. Everything that has mass pulls on everything else, and the more mass something has, the stronger its pull. «Gravitation keeps your feet on the ground.» Without it, you would float off into space.
explain: In science, gravitation is the scientific name for gravity. Isaac Newton described the law of gravitation, which explains how this pull works between objects. «The law of gravitation helps us understand the Moon's orbit.»
explain: Use context to work out the meaning. In «The Moon's gravitational pull moves the sea and makes the tides», the word gravitational comes from gravitation, so it means "caused by gravitation". Gravitation can also mean being drawn towards something, as in «a gravitation of people towards the cities», but in science it means the pull between things with mass.
fact: Gravitation is the force by which everything with mass pulls on everything else.
fact: Gravitation keeps us on the ground and keeps the Moon going round the Earth.
fact: «Gravitational» is the adjective: «gravitational pull» means the pull caused by gravitation.
fact: Isaac Newton described the law of gravitation.
fact: In everyday talk, gravitation can mean being drawn towards something, like «a gravitation of people towards cities».
exercise: «Gravitation keeps your feet on the ground.» What does gravitation do here? | It makes the ground warm | It pushes you up into the sky | It makes you lighter than air | *It pulls you towards the ground
exercise: «The tides rise and fall because of the Moon's gravitational pull.» What does gravitational mean here? | Caused by sunlight | Caused by wind | Caused by rain | *Caused by gravitation, the pull between things with mass
exercise: Which word is the adjective form of gravitation? | *Gravitational | Grave | Gravel | Gravity
test:
«Many people felt a gravitation towards the bright lights of the city.» What does gravitation mean here? | Falling down fast | Pushing away from something | *Being drawn towards something | A large ball that bounces
«Newton's law of gravitation explains how objects pull on each other.» What does this sentence tell us? | Objects are always still | Only the Moon can pull | *Objects with mass pull on each other | Objects never pull on anything
exam:
«Without gravitation, the Moon would not go round the Earth.» What does this sentence tell us? | Gravitation makes the Moon bright | The Moon is a star | *Gravitation keeps the Moon moving round the Earth | Gravitation only works in water
